config OTA_RELEASE_REPO
    string "GitHub repository to check for firmware releases"
    default "a9650615/esp32-rlcd-standalone"
    help
        "owner/name" of the repository whose latest release is offered as a
        firmware update on the settings page. Change this if you forked the
        project, or the device will offer you someone else's builds.

        The check is read-only and never installs on its own: it reports what
        it found and leaves the decision on the settings page.

        Unauthenticated GitHub API requests are rate-limited per source
        address (60/hour at the time of writing), which is ample for a check
        driven by a button press.

config OTA_RELEASE_ASSET_SUFFIX
    string "Filename suffix identifying the firmware asset in a release"
    default ".bin"
    help
        A release can carry several attachments. The first asset whose name
        ends with this suffix is treated as the firmware image.

        Note that a wrong choice here is not dangerous: whatever is downloaded
        still has to pass the image header check before a single byte of the
        OTA slot is erased.

config OTA_ALLOW_UNCONFIRMED_PUSH
    bool "Install pushed firmware without asking at the board (development)"
    default n
    help
        Skips the on-screen confirmation for POST /ota, so a push installs
        immediately.

        This exists because the confirmation costs a walk to the board on
        every single iteration, and on a device being developed over the
        network that is the slowest step in the loop by a wide margin.

        Understand exactly what it removes. The upload endpoint listens
        whenever the board is on the network and is not password-gated - the
        button press IS the authorisation. With this on, anyone who can reach
        the board's upload port can replace its firmware with no interaction
        at all. That is acceptable on a development desk and is not acceptable
        anywhere else.

        Two things keep it from escaping. It defaults to n, and it is
        additionally compiled out whenever NDEBUG is defined, so a release
        build has no bypass regardless of what this is set to. A board built
        with it on says so in its log at every boot and on the panel while it
        installs.
